        "For Step 2, I compared the mean and median of each factor for defaulted and non-defaulted observations. In general, the median differences are more reliable than the mean differences in this sample, because several variables appear to be affected by extreme values and skewed distributions. This is especially clear for variables such as debt_to_tang_assets, interest_coverage, and fcrr, where the mean differences are very large and likely distorted by outliers or very small denominators.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "The largest differences are mostly in **leverage, cash flow, and coverage-related variables**. Defaulted firms show weaker **free_cash_flow**, lower **current_ratio**, lower **dscr**, and much weaker **interest_coverage** and **fcrr**, which suggests that the main separation comes from poorer liquidity, weaker operating cash generation, and less ability to service debt. Leverage variables such as **debt_to_assets, debt_value_toa, and debt_to_tang_assets** also differ across the two groups, indicating that firms closer to default tend to carry heavier debt burdens relative to their asset base.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "The smallest differences appear in variables such as **dividends_tos**, and to some extent **goodwill_to_assets**, which do not separate defaulted and non-defaulted firms very clearly on their own. I think the main reason is that these variables are either less directly tied to near-term default risk or are affected by firm-specific accounting choices rather than immediate financial distress. I would also note that some of the mean differences are likely distorted by outliers, so the median differences are more reliable for interpretation in this sample."
